    costume: 'A loose white smock and trousers and a tall, soft, conical hat, often with a hunched back or a pot belly beneath.',
    sense: 'Old bread and sour wine, thick and hard to shift. The animal is a chicken that has survived several attempts on its life and expects another.',
    wants: 'Food, comfort, and a way out of whatever trouble he is in.',
    fears: 'Work, danger and consequences.',
    contradiction: 'Clever and stupid, cruel and pitiful, bully and victim, all at once.',
    centre: 'The belly and the rounded back: a contradictory torso that seems heavy and restless at the same time.',
    leads: 'The belly when he is idle, and the beak of the nose the moment something interests him.',
    stance: ['Feet apart, weight sinking.', 'Knees soft.', 'Pelvis tucked under a forward belly.', 'Back rounded.', 'Head thrust forward on the neck.', 'Arms loose.', 'Attention darting and suspicious.'],
    walk: 'Two speeds: a heavy, shuffling amble, and sudden jerky dashes led by the head, like a hen’s. The body would rather be sitting down until the head decides otherwise.',
    principle: 'His body is lazy until his head wants something, and then everything follows the beak.',
    thinking: 'He argues with himself: two quick opinions pulling in two directions, the head jerking between them, until he picks whichever one gets him out of trouble.',
    fearResponse: 'He hides behind someone he was threatening a moment ago.',
    desireResponse: 'He denies wanting what he is already taking.',
    gestures: ['A pecking head.', 'Shrugging off blame with the whole body.', 'Pointing at somebody else.', 'Patting his belly in satisfaction.', 'A stick raised, then hidden behind his back.'],
    audience: 'He argues with the audience, justifies himself to them, and asks them to agree that none of it was his fault.',
    signature: ['He steals food.', 'He is caught.', 'He denies it with his mouth full.', 'He blames the one who caught him.', 'He finishes the food.'],
    recovery: 'He changes his story, and sometimes his side, and carries on as though he had always been right.',
    voice: 'One playable interpretation: high, sharp, nasal and argumentative, rising quickly into complaint. His English puppet descendant, Punch, took the squawk to an extreme, with a small reed held in the puppeteer’s mouth.',
