export const CHARACTER_LESSONS = [
  { id: 'ch-cm-why', module: 'commedia', order: 1,
    title: 'Why We Start With Commedia',
    attribution: 'Speechcraft original',
    requiredReviewer: 'acting-professional',
    objective: 'Understand why this course begins with characters that already exist, and how studying them prepares you to build your own.',
    orientation: 'Welcome to a craft that has transcended languages, cultures and centuries.',
    reflection: 'Think of a character you would love to play one day. Which parts of them could you describe right now, and which would you have to guess?',
    body: [
      { p: 'Masked players carried these characters from one town square to the next, across Italy and far beyond it, and audiences who did not share a language laughed at the same faces. The masks have outlived every actor who ever wore them, and they are still here, waiting for whoever puts them on next. Today, that is you.' },
      { p: 'We start with commedia because it gives you a cast of preloaded characters that have existed for centuries and always will. It takes the work out of building a brand-new, multidimensional character, which cuts the learning curve, so you can start building your own.' },
      { p: 'Each of these characters arrives complete, with everything already decided: what they want and what they fear, where they stand in their world, who they deal with and how, where their body leads from, how they walk, how they think, what fear does to them, the business they are known for, and what their words are really for. Generations of actors refined them in front of audiences until nothing unnecessary was left. They are characters with the workings on the outside.' },
      { p: 'That makes them the best teachers you could have. When you study Pantalone, you are not only learning an old Venetian merchant. You are learning what a centre of gravity is, what it means for a nose to lead, how a want turns into a walk, and how a person puts themselves back together after being caught out. Every one of those is a question you will ask of every character you ever build.' },
      { p: 'Much of what the Acting course teaches you to dig for is already here, in plain sight. What this person wants. What they are afraid of losing. The contradiction they carry. Who they are to everyone else, and where the power sits between them. How they move, how they speak, and what their words are really for. In most storytelling you have to excavate all of that. In commedia the characters walk on wearing it.' },
      { p: 'The questions that belong to the scene rather than the person are answered too: the obstacle in the way tonight, what has just changed, what it will cost. Those live in the scenario, the outline of events every actor knows before the show begins. Nothing is hidden from the performer, which is what makes this such a good place to learn.' },
      { h: 'How this course uses them' },
      { steps: [
        'First you learn the characters as they are: the history, the form, and each mask in turn, broken down part by part.',
        'Then you play them: find the stance, the walk and the signature business in your own body, and practise with Mask Cards and One Line, Many Masks.',
        'Then you build your own. In My Characters, the same breakdown becomes a worksheet: first for your own version of a mask, then for a person you invent, and for a role you are cast in.',
      ] },
      { p: 'By the time you reach the later modules, inventing a person from scratch or building one from a script, you will already know what a character is made of, because you will have taken nine of them apart.' },
    ] },

  { id: 'ch-cm-history', module: 'commedia', order: 2,
    title: 'The History of Commedia',
    attribution: 'Commedia tradition',
    requiredReviewer: 'acting-professional',
    objective: 'Know where commedia came from, how its companies worked, what structured improvisation meant, and where its characters went after it faded.',
    orientation: 'Commedia was not actors making it all up. It was trained professionals improvising inside a form they knew completely.',
    reflection: 'The companies improvised inside a plot everyone already knew. Where in your own work do you know the destination but find the route fresh each time?',
    body: [
      { p: 'Commedia dell’arte was professional popular theatre that grew up in Italy in the 1500s. Touring companies played recurring characters, most of them masked, and built each performance from a prepared plot outline, improvising much of the dialogue on the night. The word arte here means craft or trade, in the way a guild is a trade. A fair translation is “the comedy of the professionals”: it separated working actors, who lived by their skill, from the courtly amateurs who performed for pleasure.' },
      { p: 'Those amateurs had a comedy of their own, and it had a name: commedia erudita, the learned comedy. It was written down, it followed classical models closely enough that catching the references took an education, and it was staged indoors for invited guests at courts and academies, often performed by members of the court themselves. Even Machiavelli wrote one. It is also where the scenery revolution happened, with painted perspective sets that only resolved correctly from the centre of the room, where the ruler sat. That is what commedia dell’arte set itself against: one comedy written down and played indoors for people who had been invited, the other improvised in the open for anyone who paid.' },
      { p: 'Nobody invented it. It grew out of everything around it: the old Roman comedies of lovers, fathers and clever slaves, the masked Atellan farces of southern Italy, carnival and marketplace performers, jesters, acrobats and musicians, and the rivalries between Italy’s cities.' },
      { p: 'Of all those ancestors, the Atellan farces are the ones scholars reach for most often, because they are the closest in kind. They were largely improvised, they used masks, and their cast was a set of fixed types: a foolish old man, a boastful chatterer, a greedy clown and a cunning schemer. Read that list beside the nine characters in this module and the resemblance is hard to miss. What nobody can show is the line between them. More than a thousand years separate the two, with very little evidence of what happened in between, so treat this as the leading theory about where commedia came from rather than a documented descent.' },
      { p: 'The earliest surviving contract for a professional acting company was signed in Padua in 1545, when eight men agreed to tour together, perform comedies and share the money. That date does not mark the beginning of commedia. It marks the moment actors began organising their work as a business. What made that worth doing was the world around it. Every other trade was organising into guilds with charters and recognised rights, while players, jugglers and acrobats were still treated by the Church as disreputable and pushed toward the margins of respectable life. What the Church objected to was real enough: the comedy was frankly bawdy, the playing was physical and close to the body, and from the 1560s there were women on stage doing both. Going to a notary put acting on the other side of that line: a trade with terms, obligations and a share of the takings, held in law, in the way that being a blacksmith was a trade.' },
      { p: 'For most of that first century there were no theatres to play in. Purpose-built houses barely existed, court performances went up inside palace walls on stages struck as soon as the evening ended, and a touring company worked in the open: a platform in a city square, a curtain behind it, and two painted wings an actor could walk through. That was the whole set, and everything else travelled with them. Only as the number of professional companies grew did small paying playhouses appear, with an impresario arranging which town and which room came next. A square is a hard room. Nothing dims, nothing quietens a crowd, and nobody has paid to stay. The size of commedia was built for exactly that.' },
      { p: 'Italy was not yet one country. It was a patchwork of city-states, duchies and kingdoms, each with its own dialect and its own reputation, and commedia turned that into character. Pantalone spoke Venetian and was a Venetian merchant. The Doctor came from Bologna, a university city. Many of the servants came from the hills around Bergamo. The lovers spoke polished literary Italian. An audience knew who a character was, where they came from and where they stood in the world before the plot had explained anything. The way a person spoke was part of their costume.' },
      { p: 'Actors often played one character for their whole career, building up a personal stock of speeches, jokes, songs, insults, love declarations and physical routines for it. The verbal half of that stock had a name, the generici, and it was general on purpose: speeches built to serve in any comedy, carried from one play into the next across a whole career. So when a company improvised, nobody started from nothing. Every actor knew the plot, every character’s want, the entrances and exits, the turns the story had to take and how it had to end. What they found fresh each night was the route. The plot said what happened. The actors discovered how it happened tonight.' },
      { p: 'Women reached the professional stage in Italy earlier than anywhere else in Europe. Before the 1560s they were barred from it, and women’s parts went to men. Then, on 10 October 1564, a contract signed in Rome listed Lucrezia di Siena among a company of professional players: the earliest woman known by name to have been hired as an actress. Companies settled at around ten performers, at least two of them women, and within a generation the best actresses were among the most celebrated performers in Europe. It is worth holding that date beside another one. Shakespeare was born the same year, and on the English stage his women would be played by boys for most of the next hundred years.' },
      { p: 'Companies toured to France, Spain and the courts of northern Europe, playing to audiences who did not understand Italian. That pushed the work toward physical clarity: an audience that missed every word could still see that a servant was starving, lying or terrified. The intention carried the meaning. It still does.' },
      { h: 'What commedia became' },
      { p: 'Italian companies were a sensation in France, where they worked for generations and changed French comedy for good. Molière learned from them, and his scheming servants and furious misers are commedia masks in French clothes. The Scenes shelf has one: in Tartuffe, the maid Dorine tells her master’s daughter the truth nobody else will, which is Colombina exactly, and the daughter, Mariane, is a lover who cannot solve her own problem. The Italians were expelled from Paris in 1697 and invited back in 1716. By then their characters had new names across Europe: Arlecchino became Harlequin, Pedrolino became Pierrot, Colombina became Columbine, Scaramuccia became Scaramouche, Pantalone became Pantaloon, and Pulcinella became Punch.' },
      { p: 'In eighteenth-century Venice, two playwrights pulled commedia in opposite directions. Carlo Goldoni wrote full scripts that turned the masks into particular, recognisable people; his The Servant of Two Masters keeps the old energy inside a written play and is still performed everywhere. Carlo Gozzi defended the masks, the fantasy and the improvisation, and set commedia’s characters loose in fairy-tale worlds. Between them they show the two things you can do with a tradition: make it more real, or make it stranger.' },
      { p: 'As the leading form of popular theatre, commedia faded during the 1700s. Its characters did not. They moved into pantomime and puppetry, circus clowning, opera and ballet, music hall and vaudeville, silent film, animation, sketch comedy and the sitcom. The hungry servant who takes every instruction literally, the pompous expert, the miser, the braggart, the lovestruck couple and the clever maid who runs the household are all still working.' },
    ] },

  { id: 'ch-cm-slapstick', module: 'commedia', order: 3,
    title: 'Slapstick',
    attribution: 'Commedia tradition',
    requiredReviewer: 'acting-professional',
    objective: 'Know what slapstick is, where the word comes from, how a gag is built, and where to watch it done brilliantly.',
    orientation: 'Slapstick is the comedy of the body going wrong. It looks like chaos and is some of the most precise work an actor ever does.',
    reflection: 'Think of the last time you laughed at someone falling over in a film. What was really hurt: their body, or their dignity?',
    body: [
      { p: 'Slapstick is physical comedy built on exaggerated, harmless mishap: slips, trips, falls, blows, collisions, chases, and objects that refuse to cooperate. The name comes straight from commedia. Arlecchino’s bat, the batacchio, was two thin slats of wood joined so that they slapped together with a loud crack on impact. A blow that barely touched another actor sounded tremendous. So the first slapstick was a safety device: the sound sold the hit, and nobody was hurt.' },
      { p: 'That is still the rule. Slapstick is funny only when the audience knows, somewhere underneath the laugh, that no one is really hurt. What gets hurt is dignity. The pompous man who slips on a banana skin is funnier than a child who does, because he had further to fall. That is why slapstick and status belong together: almost every physical gag is a status gag in disguise.' },
      { h: 'How a gag is built' },
      { steps: [
        'The setup: the audience sees the problem before the character does, the banana skin, the open trapdoor, the wet paint.',
        'The anticipation: a beat in which the audience knows what is coming and waits for it.',
        'The event: the slip, the bump or the crash, as clean and exact as a dance step.',
        'The take: the reaction. Audiences laugh at the reaction more than at the event, so give it room.',
        'The escalation: the same thing again, bigger, often three times, with a twist on the third.',
      ] },
      { p: 'Two rules matter more than the rest. Commit completely: the character never knows it is funny, and the moment you comment on the joke, it dies. And never improvise contact. Every fall, hit and collision you see on screen was choreographed and rehearsed, often by trained stunt performers, and falls in particular need proper training. You can learn most of slapstick’s craft, the timing and the take, without them.' },
      { h: 'Where to see it' },
      { list: [
        'The Gold Rush (1925): snowbound and starving, Charlie Chaplin’s Tramp boils his boot and eats it like a feast, twirling the laces like spaghetti.',
        'Safety Last! (1923): Harold Lloyd dangles from the hands of a clock high above the street.',
        'Steamboat Bill, Jr. (1928): the front of a house falls on Buster Keaton, who survives because he is standing exactly where the open window lands.',
        'The Music Box (1932): Laurel and Hardy try to deliver a piano up an enormous flight of outdoor steps, and it keeps coming back down.',
        'Singin’ in the Rain (1952): in the number “Make ’Em Laugh”, Donald O’Connor runs up walls, fights a dummy and crashes through a set.',
        'I Love Lucy, “Job Switching” (1952): Lucy and Ethel on a chocolate factory conveyor belt that speeds up faster than they can wrap.',
        'Fawlty Towers, “Gourmet Night” (1975): Basil Fawlty’s car breaks down, and he gives it a thrashing with a tree branch.',
        'Mr. Bean, “Merry Christmas, Mr. Bean” (1992): Mr. Bean gets his head stuck inside a turkey.',
        'Home Alone (1990): two burglars break into a house full of traps, from paint cans swinging down the stairs to a red-hot doorknob.',
        'The Road Runner cartoons: Wile E. Coyote and the anvils, rockets and cliff edges that betray him every time.',
      ] },
      { h: 'Try it: the take' },
      { p: 'No falls needed. Stand easy and read an imaginary letter. The news in it is terrible, but do not react yet. Fold it, put it away, take one step, then stop: that is the take, the moment it finally lands. Now try the double take: glance at the news, look away as though it were nothing, and snap back to it. Now the slow burn: let it land so slowly that the audience watches your fury build for five whole seconds before you speak. Three reactions to the same event, and each one gets a different laugh.' },
    ] },

  { id: 'ch-cm-scenario', module: 'commedia', order: 4,
    title: 'The Scenario',
    attribution: 'Commedia tradition',
    requiredReviewer: 'acting-professional',
    objective: 'Understand how a commedia company improvised a whole play from a one-page outline, and try it.',
    orientation: 'The scenario said what happened. The actors decided how it happened tonight.',
    reflection: 'The ending was fixed before you began. Did knowing it make the scenes feel more free or less free?',
    body: [
      { p: 'A commedia company could perform a written play, and its repertoire included them: pastoral dramas, and comedies and tragedies by Latin and contemporary authors. What these companies were famous for was the other thing they could do. An improvised comedy was built from a scenario, sometimes called a canovaccio: a short outline of the plot, scene by scene, saying who comes on, what happens and who leaves. The outline was often pinned up backstage, and the actors improvised the dialogue as they went. A printed collection of fifty scenarios survives from 1611, and reading it is a strange experience. A whole evening’s comedy fits on a couple of pages. A treatise published in 1699 goes further. It sets down how acting was done both from a written text and from improvisation, and it describes the principal masks, which makes it the closest thing the form has to a manual written from the inside.' },
      { p: 'It worked because nothing essential was left to chance. Every actor knew their own character inside out, knew what every other character wanted, knew the status of everyone on stage, and carried a stock of prepared speeches and comic routines, called lazzi. The scenario supplied the events: the discoveries, the reversals, the ending. Commedia was not unstructured improvisation. It was disciplined freedom inside a known form.' },
      { h: 'A scenario to try' },
      { list: [
        'Two young lovers want to marry.',
        'Pantalone plans to marry his daughter to the rich old Doctor instead.',
        'The lovers beg the servants for help.',
        'Brighella forges a legal document to stop the wedding.',
        'Arlecchino delivers it to the wrong person.',
        'Il Capitano arrives, threatens everyone, and runs from the first real danger.',
        'Colombina exposes the whole mess.',
        'The old men are defeated, the lovers marry, and the servants get food, money or love.',
      ] },
      { p: 'With a group, give each person one character, read the scenario aloud once, and play it straight through, improvising every word. On your own, take one step at a time and improvise it from each character’s side in turn. Either way, keep one rule: you must arrive at the next step, but you may take any route to get there.' },
    ] },

  { id: 'ch-cm-form', module: 'commedia', order: 5,
    title: 'Learning a Form',
    attribution: 'Speechcraft original',
    requiredReviewer: 'acting-professional',
    objective: 'Understand what it means to play a character that exists before you do, and how to learn one.',
    orientation: 'Most characters you build start as a blank. These arrive finished. Your job is to learn them exactly, then bring them to life.',
    reflection: 'Think of a role you have played or watched. What did that character want their words to do to the person listening?',
    body: [
      { p: 'There are three kinds of character work. You can invent a person from scratch. You can build a person a writer created, working from the script. Or you can learn a person the tradition created, and that is what commedia dell’arte asks of you. Arlecchino existed for centuries before you arrived: his hunger, his crouch, his speed, his black half mask, the way he misunderstands every order. You do not decide who he is. You find out, and then you become him.' },
      { p: 'That sounds like less freedom. It is a different kind. A commedia actor learns the form so completely that it stops being a costume and becomes a way of moving and thinking. Once the form is in the body, everything inside it is yours: the timing, the invention, the particular way your Arlecchino wants his lunch tonight. Precision first, then freedom. The form is the floor you stand on, not the ceiling.' },
      { p: 'A commedia character is not a costume or a personality. It is a repeatable system that connects desire, status, speech and action, and every character in this module is broken down the same way:' },
      { list: [
        'Their family and status: how much physical and social space they believe they are entitled to.',
        'What they want, what they fear, and the contradiction at their core.',
        'A sensory image to experiment with: a taste, a texture, an animal. These are provocations to try in the body, not facts about the character, and you are free to replace any of them with one that works better for you.',
        'Their centre of gravity, and the part of them that enters a room first.',
        'Their base stance: the physical home they return to after every interruption.',
        'Their walk, as a rhythm rather than a pose.',
        'How a thought travels through their body, and what fear and desire do to it.',
        'Their gestures, their relationship with the audience, and their signature business.',
        'How they recover when they are caught out.',
        'Their voice and breath, and what their words are really for.',
      ] },
      { p: 'One question sits under all of it, and this whole module keeps asking it: what does this person want their words to accomplish? Pantalone does not talk about money to share information. He talks about money to own you. The Doctor does not explain things to teach you. He explains them to be seen as the cleverest person in the room. Commedia made those strategies so clear that an audience could read them across a crowded square, which is why it still teaches actors. Every mask is a way of using speech to get something.' },
      { p: 'The characters come in four families, and the module meets them from the top down: the old masters, who own the money and the rules; the young lovers, who want each other and little else; the braggart soldier, who talks bravely and runs; and the servants, who carry the plot and wreck it. Learn the families first. Individual names changed from company to company and city to city, but the families held.' },
      { h: 'Masks in everyday speech' },
      { p: 'The masks are still working in ordinary speech too, and this may be the most useful thing commedia can teach a speaker. One person can be several masks in a single day. Talking about money, they are Pantalone. Explaining something they do not understand, they are the Doctor. Describing their own leadership, they are the Captain. Asking for forgiveness, they are Pedrolino. The mask is not who someone is. It is the strategy they reach for when something they want is at stake.' },
      { h: 'One line, many masks' },
      { p: 'Say this line: “I came because I thought you might need me.” Now say it as each mask, keeping every word the same:' },
      { list: [
        'Pantalone: my coming puts you in my debt.',
        'Il Dottore: I understood the situation better than anyone.',
        'Il Capitano: I alone was brave enough to come.',
        'A lover: fate itself sent me.',
        'Arlecchino: I may have come to the wrong house.',
        'Brighella: I want you to believe this helps you.',
        'Pulcinella: I want nothing at all, and I clearly want something.',
        'Pedrolino: I care, and I am risking rejection by saying so.',
        'Colombina: I saw what you needed, so I came.',
      ] },
      { p: 'The words never change. The want underneath them does, and it changes the body, the rhythm and the voice along with it. That is the whole of commedia in a single sentence.' },
      { h: 'Playing with them, not at them' },
      { p: 'Commedia is played with an audience rather than in front of one. That began as a necessity. In a square where nothing dims and nobody has paid to stay, a performer who ignores the room loses it. What the necessity produced is a habit worth keeping: the performer watches the audience as closely as the audience watches the performer.' },
      { p: 'The loop runs like this. You make an offer: a look, a line, a piece of business. The room answers, with laughter, or silence, or the particular quiet of people who have stopped following. You listen to that answer. You adjust the size, the speed or the aim. Then you make the next offer. None of that changes the story you are telling. All of it changes how you tell it tonight.' },
      { p: 'The principle underneath is larger than commedia. Saying a thing is not the same as being understood. A speech is not finished because you reached the end of it, and a scene is not working because you played it correctly. It is finished when it has landed on the person in front of you, and the only way to know whether it has is to keep looking at them while you speak.' },
      { h: 'What is history, and what is craft' },
      { p: 'Commedia never had one fixed cast or one fixed way of moving. Companies, cities and generations renamed, combined and reinvented the characters, and very little about how they moved was written down. So this module keeps five kinds of knowledge apart:' },
      { list: [
        'Documented history: what contracts, printed scenarios, letters and pictures from the period actually show.',
        'Longstanding convention: what performers have handed down and is traditionally associated with a character, such as Pantalone’s red costume or Arlecchino’s bat.',
        'Modern training: movement vocabulary that twentieth-century teachers reconstructed and organised, commonly used in commedia training today.',
        'Speechcraft’s interpretation: playable ways into a character offered by this course, one choice among many.',
        'Received wisdom: what popular accounts repeat about commedia. Often right, occasionally badly wrong, and never a source. This course treats it as something to check rather than something to cite.',
      ] },
      { p: 'Here is that last distinction doing real work. A widely watched introduction to commedia will tell you that Arlecchino wears a green mask. Ask what kind of claim it is. The surviving pictures and the handed-down convention agree that his mask is black, and that green belongs to Brighella, whose olive leather is one of the things you identify him by. So the green Arlecchino is not documented history and not longstanding convention. It is received wisdom, repeated with enough confidence to sound settled. You will meet a great deal of it, and the question is always the same: which of these five is this, and who would actually know?' },
      { p: 'Every character chapter ends with a note saying which is which. Treat each walk and sequence as a way in, not a rule: performance conventions vary by company, teacher, period and region.' },
      { h: 'Questions to ask of every mask' },
      { list: [
        'What reaches the listener first: the person’s words, or their status?',
        'Which part of the body starts the thought?',
        'What does the character protect?',
        'What makes the character speed up?',
        'What happens to the body when the character is afraid?',
        'Does the body support the words, or contradict them?',
        'What does the character want the audience to believe?',
        'What happens when their usual strategy fails?',
        'Which active verb best describes what they are doing right now?',
        'How would the same sentence change if it were played through another mask?',
      ] },
    ] },

  { id: 'ch-archetypes', module: 'commedia', order: 6, movesFrom: 'ac-archetypes',
    title: 'What Is an Archetype',
    attribution: 'Archetype tradition',
    requiredReviewer: 'acting-professional',
    objective: 'Recognize archetypes: the characters every culture already knows.',
    orientation: 'Some characters exist everywhere. Change the country, change the language, change the century, and there they still are.',
    reflection: 'Which archetype do you play without trying? And which one have you never once been cast as?',
    body: [
      { p: 'Certain characters show up in every tradition that has ever put people in front of an audience. The braggart who folds the moment he is tested. The miser. The trickster servant who is smarter than his master. The young lovers that everything conspires against. The tyrant. The fool who is the only one telling the truth. Italian street theatre had them in masks: commedia dell’arte built a whole tradition on them. Sanskrit drama had them. Shadow plays had them. Looney Tunes has them. SpongeBob has them. Your favorite sitcom has them tonight. Different names, different clothes, same people.' },
      { p: 'They survive because they cross every barrier. An audience that shares none of your language still recognizes the braggart within seconds, because they grew up with their own. That recognition is money in the bank: the audience meets you halfway before you have earned anything. It also explains what happens when you improvise. The people who step forward out of you tend to be archetypes wearing specifics, because the universal cast is the deepest layer of everything you absorbed.' },
      { p: 'Use them as skeletons, never as cages. An archetype is where a character starts, not where it ends. Play the type alone and you have a cartoon that anyone could do. Build the specifics on top, this braggart, from this place, with this wound, and you have a person the audience recognizes instantly and has still never met. Recognized at first sight, surprising ever after. That is the pairing to aim for.' },
    ] },

  { id: 'ch-cm-mask', module: 'commedia', order: 7,
    title: 'The Mask Leads',
    attribution: 'Modern commedia training',
    requiredReviewer: 'acting-professional',
    objective: 'Understand how a mask changes the way an actor moves, and practise letting it lead.',
    orientation: 'A mask does not hide you. It takes your face away, so the rest of your body has to do the talking.',
    reflection: 'When you tried the imagined mask, which part of you gave it away first: the head, the shoulders, the hands or the feet?',
    body: [
      { p: 'Most of commedia’s comic characters wore a half mask covering the forehead, eyes and nose, leaving the mouth free to speak. The lovers played unmasked, and so did a few others. A mask is not a disguise. It fixes one expression in place, permanently, and that changes everything below it. With the face frozen, the character has to speak through the head, the spine, the hips, the hands, the rhythm of moving and stopping, the distance kept from other people, and the voice.' },
      { p: 'That is why masked actors learn to lead with the head. A small turn of the head reads as a look; a sharp one reads as a thought arriving. The mask always faces something, and whatever it faces, the audience looks at too. Stillness becomes loud. A mask that stops dead in the middle of a scramble can get a bigger laugh than any line.' },
      { p: 'A mask also exposes a gap you can use in any role: the body often says what the words are trying to hide. A masked braggart can boast in the bravest voice he owns while his knees are already turning toward the exit. The audience believes the knees.' },
      { p: 'One part of this only appears when there are other people on stage. The unmasked characters never treat a mask as a mask. To the lovers, Pantalone is not a performer in moulded leather. He is an old man who will not let them marry. Everyone plays the masked characters as ordinary people, and that is what lets the audience hold both facts at once: that it is obviously a piece of leather, and that it is a person. A mask becomes real because the company behaves as though it already is.' },
      { h: 'From shape to speech' },
      { p: 'There is an order to this work, and it runs upward from the body. A shape comes first. The shape changes the breath, because a braced chest and a lifted one cannot take the same one. The breath, once you let it be heard, is already most of a voice. The voice finds words, and the words go to somebody who would rather not hear them. Work in that order and the voice arrives attached to a body that earned it.' },
      { p: 'Work in the other order and it shows. An actor who settles on a funny voice first and then hunts for a walk to go with it is wearing two things that never quite belong to the same person. The breath is the hinge between them, the one thing that is part of the body and part of the speech at once, which is why every breakdown in this module treats voice and breath as a single entry rather than two.' },
      { h: 'Too much, then less' },
      { p: 'Start bigger than feels reasonable. Make the shape unmistakable, take the breath further than you would, let the rhythm go to an extreme, and commit to all of it completely. Nothing can be discovered from a version so careful that nothing happened. Then find out whether it reads: ask somebody what they saw, or watch yourself back. Only once it is legible do you begin taking away, removing whatever is decorating rather than doing, and keeping whatever the character stops working without.' },
      { p: 'Both halves are the work, and most actors only do one. Commit without ever cutting and you have an impression, loud and approximate. Cut without ever committing and there was nothing there to refine in the first place. The order matters too: you cannot sculpt what you have not made yet.' },
      { h: 'Try it: the imagined mask' },
      { p: 'You do not need a real mask for this. Start in neutral: feet under your hips, weight even, face relaxed, looking straight ahead. Now imagine a mask settling onto your face with one fixed expression, suspicious, say, or starving, or delighted with itself. Keep your own face still and let the expression travel down. How does a suspicious head sit on the neck? What do suspicious shoulders do? Walk. Stop. Turn your head toward something and let the whole body follow a moment later. Now stand still and listen to your breathing. The shape has already changed it. Let it be audible, let the sound of it become a hum and then a word, and say that word to somebody across the room. Notice that you did not choose the voice. Then take the mask off, come back to neutral, and try another.' },
      { p: 'Work gently and inside what your body allows today. None of this needs falls, jumps or deep crouches to be useful, and nothing here should hurt.' },
    ] },

  { id: 'ch-cm-cast', module: 'commedia', order: 8,
    attribution: 'Commedia tradition',
    requiredReviewer: 'acting-professional',
    title: 'The Cast',
    objective: 'Meet the whole company at once: four families, nine characters, and who deals with whom.',
    orientation: 'Before you take them apart one at a time, look at the company as a whole.',
    reflection: 'Which of the nine do you already recognise from something you have watched this year?',
    body: [
      { p: 'Here is the whole company on one page. Four families, nine characters, and a set of relationships that has driven comedies for four hundred years. Every chapter after this one takes a single character apart. This is the map you will be filling in.' },
      { fig: 'cm-cast-tree' },
      { h: 'The old masters, the vecchi' },
      { p: 'They own the money, the houses and the rules, and in most plots they are the obstacle.' },
      { roster: ['pantalone', 'dottore'] },
      { h: 'The lovers, the innamorati' },
      { p: 'The reason the plot exists, and the people least able to do anything about it.' },
      { roster: ['innamorati'] },
      { h: 'The outsider, Il Capitano' },
      { p: 'He arrives from somewhere else, claims the highest status on stage, and has none.' },
      { roster: ['capitano'] },
      { h: 'The servants, the zanni' },
      { p: 'The play runs on them. They carry the letters, invent the schemes, wreck the plans and eat whatever is left. They divide into two jobs: a first zanni who schemes and calculates, and a second zanni who blunders and follows his appetite. Brighella and Arlecchino are the classic pair, and their chapters take each job apart.' },
      { roster: ['brighella', 'arlecchino', 'colombina', 'pulcinella', 'pedrolino'] },
      { h: 'Who deals with whom' },
      { list: [
        'The masters employ the servants, underpay them, and depend on them completely.',
        'The lovers are the masters’ children, and they need the servants for every letter, meeting and rescue.',
        'Brighella makes the plans; Arlecchino carries them out and ruins them.',
        'Colombina sees through everyone, and is courted by Arlecchino and loved by Pedrolino.',
        'Il Capitano courts the young woman, bullies the servants, and folds the moment anyone tests him.',
        'Pantalone and Il Dottore are old friends, rivals, and each other’s intended in-laws.',
      ] },
      { h: 'The same jobs under other names' },
      { p: 'There is no single official list of commedia characters. Companies invented, renamed and combined them for four hundred years, and every region added its own: Meneghino in Milan, Gianduja in Turin, Stenterello in Florence, Rugantino in Rome, Peppe Nappa in Sicily, and dozens more. The nine above are the ones this course takes apart, because between them they cover every family and every job in a plot. Most of the other names you will meet are these same jobs wearing different labels, which is worth knowing when you open a play or read a museum card:' },
      { list: [
        'Truffaldino: Arlecchino’s job in Carlo Goldoni’s The Servant of Two Masters. Serving two masters is a plot, not a different person.',
        'Scapino: Brighella’s job in Molière’s Scapin the Schemer, played more openly as a trickster and less as a threat.',
        'Scaramuccia: a real difference. He carries the Captain’s swagger with actual skill and charm, so where Il Capitano collapses, he talks and fights his way out. He became the French Scaramouche.',
        'Smeraldina: Colombina’s job in The Servant of Two Masters, plainer spoken and more earthy.',
        'Franceschina: Colombina’s job again, in the companies that called their servetta by that name.',
        'Balanzone: Il Dottore under his Bolognese name, the one you will see on a mask in a museum case.',
        'Tartaglia: the other real difference, a job none of the nine do. A nervous official or lawyer whose speech sticks. Traditionally the stammer was the joke, and it is not played that way now: the comedy belongs to the bureaucratic hedging, the fear of committing to an answer, and the inability to say a plain thing inside a system.',
      ] },
      { p: 'Learn the families first. Individual names changed from company to company and city to city, but the families held, and so did the jobs they do in a plot.' },
    ] },

  // ── One chapter per character (owner order 2026-09-22) ─────────
  // Each: who they are, the mask picture, the sheet, relationships, the
  // full physical breakdown (from the ONE record), stance and walk
  // pictures, a way to find it, and where you can see them today.
  { id: 'ch-cm-pantalone', module: 'commedia', order: 101, reference: true,
    title: 'Pantalone',
    attribution: 'Commedia tradition',
    requiredReviewer: 'acting-professional',
    objective: 'Learn Pantalone, the rich old Venetian who wants to acquire without risking.',
    orientation: 'Pantalone has the money. That is his answer to every question.',
    reflection: 'Pantalone keeps insisting on an authority nobody obeys any more. Where have you seen that happen?',
    body: [
      { p: 'Pantalone is the old Venetian merchant, head of the household and the richest man in most plots, and early companies also called him Il Magnifico. You will meet that older name in period scenarios, where it means this character. Some modern training traditions use it differently, for a separate figure of pure authority standing above Pantalone, so it is worth knowing which sense you are being handed. His close-fitting trousers gave English the word pantaloons. He is usually the father of one of the lovers, usually arranging a marriage the lovers do not want, and often chasing a young woman himself. His whole life fits in three words: acquire without risking.' },
      { fig: 'cm-pantalone-mask' },
      { h: 'At a glance' },
      { mask: 'pantalone' },
      { h: 'Relationships' },
      { list: [
        'The lovers: his son or daughter is one of them, and he is the obstacle between them and happiness.',
        'Il Dottore: his old friend and rival, whom he wants as a son-in-law or competes with for the same young woman.',
        'Brighella and Arlecchino: servants he underpays, mistrusts and depends on completely.',
        'Colombina: the maid he chases, who handles him with ease.',
        'Il Capitano: a suitor for his daughter, whose boasting impresses Pantalone far more than it should.',
      ] },
      { h: 'The physical breakdown' },
      { profile: 'pantalone' },
      { fig: 'cm-pantalone-stance' },
      { fig: 'cm-pantalone-walk' },
      { h: 'Find it' },
      { p: 'Hinge forward a little from the hips and let your nose lead. Take small quick steps across the room, your hands guarding an imaginary purse. Hear a coin drop somewhere: freeze, locate it with your nose, check your own purse, and dart toward it. Then imagine a stranger appearing, and contract around both the coin and your purse as you retreat, still watching.' },
      { p: 'Now hold the crouch and listen to your breath. It is small and guarded, because a body curled around a purse cannot take a generous one. Let it be heard. Let it sharpen into a voice, and say this to somebody who owes you money: “After everything I have given you, I should not have to ask twice.” Then think of the coin again and notice how much faster the breath goes.' },
      { h: 'Where you can see him' },
      { list: [
        'Harpagon in Molière’s The Miser, who loves his money more than his children.',
        'Ebenezer Scrooge in A Christmas Carol, before the ghosts arrive.',
        'Mr. Krabs in SpongeBob SquarePants, who treats every penny like family.',
        'Mr. Burns in The Simpsons, ancient, frail and convinced he owns the town.',
        'Pantaloon, his descendant in the English pantomime, the old man forever tricked by Harlequin.',
      ] },
    ] },

  { id: 'ch-cm-dottore', module: 'commedia', order: 102, reference: true,
    title: 'Il Dottore',
    attribution: 'Commedia tradition',
    requiredReviewer: 'acting-professional',
    objective: 'Learn Il Dottore, the learned man who cannot stop talking and does not know what he is talking about.',
    orientation: 'The Doctor has an answer for everything. It is always long, and it is rarely right.',
    reflection: 'One plain question can topple the Doctor. What is the plain question you would ask him?',
    body: [
      { p: 'Il Dottore comes from Bologna, home of one of Europe’s oldest universities, and he never lets anyone forget his education. He is often called Dottor Graziano, and the Bolognese version of him goes by Balanzone. He might be a physician, a lawyer or a professor, and in every version he speaks in endless explanations, dropping Latin he half remembers and technical words he does not quite understand. He shows something every speaker should notice: fluency can pass for knowledge.' },
      { fig: 'cm-dottore-mask' },
      { h: 'At a glance' },
      { mask: 'dottore' },
      { h: 'Relationships' },
      { list: [
        'Pantalone: his old friend and rival. They agree about the young, argue about everything else, and sometimes arrange for the Doctor to marry Pantalone’s daughter.',
        'The lovers: often his own son or daughter is one of them, and he lectures them instead of listening.',
        'The servants: his captive audience, who find ways to escape his speeches or turn them against him.',
        'Colombina: the person most likely to ask him the plain question he dreads.',
      ] },
      { h: 'The physical breakdown' },
      { profile: 'dottore' },
      { fig: 'cm-dottore-stance' },
      { fig: 'cm-dottore-walk' },
      { h: 'Find it' },
      { p: 'Let your weight settle back on your heels and your belly lead. Walk slowly, as if every step were worth recording. Then stop, lift one finger, and explain something you do not understand, how clouds stay up, say, at great length and with complete confidence.' },
      { p: 'Keep the weight back and the belly leading, and take a breath the size of the sentence you are about to deliver. Spend it slowly, as though the air itself were expensive. Let it carry you into “What you have failed to account for is the underlying multifactorial nature of the paradigm,” and do not stop where a full stop would go.' },
      { h: 'Where you can see him' },
      { list: [
        'Polonius in Hamlet, who gives endless advice and never quite reaches the point.',
        'Dr. Pangloss in Voltaire’s Candide, who can explain why every disaster is for the best.',
        'Frasier Crane in Frasier, whose learning never saves him from a single mistake.',
        'Cliff Clavin in Cheers, the barstool expert on everything.',
      ] },
    ] },

  { id: 'ch-cm-lovers', module: 'commedia', order: 103, reference: true,
    title: 'The Lovers',
    attribution: 'Commedia tradition',
    requiredReviewer: 'acting-professional',
    objective: 'Learn the innamorati: unmasked, beautifully spoken, completely sincere, and almost useless.',
    orientation: 'The lovers are the reason the plot exists, and the people least able to do anything about it.',
    reflection: 'Say the lovers’ line twice: once as a joke, once completely sincere. Which one was funnier?',
    body: [
      { p: 'The innamorati are the young lovers, and every commedia plot is, on the surface, about getting them together. They speak the most polished Italian on the stage, full of poetry and elaborate declarations. Their names changed from company to company: Flavio, Lelio, Leandro and Orazio; Isabella, Flaminia, Silvia and Lavinia.' },
      { p: 'There is more than one generation of them. The young pair are the ones most companies played and the ones this chapter breaks down: educated on their fathers’ money, fluent in the language of adulthood well before they understand what it costs. Some traditions also carry an older pair, grown aristocrats with real power and the capacity to use it badly. The difference is the one between Romeo and Juliet and the Macbeths. Same unmasked face, same beautiful speech, entirely different stakes.' },
      { fig: 'cm-innamorata-mask' },
      { fig: 'cm-innamorato-mask' },
      { h: 'At a glance' },
      { mask: 'innamorati' },
      { h: 'Relationships' },
      { list: [
        'Each other: the whole point. Every plot is, on the surface, about getting them together.',
        'Pantalone and Il Dottore: their fathers, and the obstacle. The old men want a different marriage, usually for money.',
        'Colombina, Brighella and Arlecchino: the servants they depend on for letters, meetings and rescues.',
        'Il Capitano: the rival suitor, loud and hollow, whom the servants have to get rid of.',
      ] },
      { h: 'The physical breakdown' },
      { profile: 'innamorati' },
      { fig: 'cm-innamorata-stance' },
      { fig: 'cm-innamorata-walk' },
      { fig: 'cm-innamorato-stance' },
      { fig: 'cm-innamorato-walk' },
      { h: 'Find it' },
      { p: 'Stand tall with your chest lifted and one foot slightly forward, as if about to dance. Say the lovers’ line to an empty chair as though it held the most beautiful person alive. Then hear an imaginary father’s footsteps and let the whole pose collapse into despair, and rebuild it the moment he has gone.' },
      { p: 'Stay in the pose and let the breath climb into the top of your chest, where feeling lives and good sense does not. Let it come out as one long phrase rather than several short ones. Say “You are the only person who has ever truly seen me,” and mean every syllable of it. The comedy is not in mocking the feeling. It is in how completely you mean it.' },
      { p: 'The trap is to play the lovers as a joke. They are funny because they are completely sincere. Their feelings are real, even when their idea of love has been assembled from poems, songs and daydreams, and even when they seem half in love with the sound of their own declarations. The comedy is that the audience can see how little they have actually seen of each other.' },
      { h: 'Where you can see them' },
      { list: [
        'Romeo and Juliet, who fall in love in a single scene.',
        'The four young lovers of A Midsummer Night’s Dream, chasing each other through the woods.',
        'Valère and Mariane in Molière’s Tartuffe, whose quarrel the maid Dorine has to fix.',
        'The couple at the centre of almost every romantic comedy, kept apart until the final scene.',
      ] },
    ] },

  { id: 'ch-cm-capitano', module: 'commedia', order: 104, reference: true,
    title: 'Il Capitano',
    attribution: 'Commedia tradition',
    requiredReviewer: 'acting-professional',
    objective: 'Learn Il Capitano, whose courage exists only in the words he uses about himself.',
    orientation: 'The Captain is enormous until the moment he is tested. Then he disappears.',
    reflection: 'Where in your own life have you heard someone describe a courage they never had to prove?',
    body: [
      { p: 'Il Capitano is the boastful soldier, a type as old as Roman comedy, telling stories of battles he never fought. His names say it all: Spavento means terror, Fracasso means smash, and Matamoros means killer of Moors. Other companies called him Rodomonte, Spezzaferro, Rinoceronte or Coccodrillo. In the years when Spain ruled much of Italy he was often played as a Spanish officer, and local audiences enjoyed watching the occupier’s swagger collapse.' },
      { fig: 'cm-capitano-mask' },
      { h: 'At a glance' },
      { mask: 'capitano' },
      { h: 'Relationships' },
      { list: [
        'The lovers: often a rival suitor for the young woman, loud where her true love is sincere.',
        'Pantalone: easily impressed by the Captain’s titles, and ready to hand him a daughter.',
        'Arlecchino and the servants: the people he bullies, and the people who expose him.',
        'Colombina: the woman he boasts at, who is never once impressed.',
      ] },
      { h: 'The physical breakdown' },
      { profile: 'capitano' },
      { fig: 'cm-capitano-stance' },
      { fig: 'cm-capitano-walk' },
      { h: 'Find it' },
      { p: 'Cross the room as the Captain describing a battle, chest high, arms wide, voice ringing. Then imagine a small dog barking at your heels, and let the whole body shrink in an instant, keeping your feet under you. When the danger has passed, puff back up and carry on the story as if nothing happened. The faster and more complete the collapse, the funnier it is. The question that punctures him is always the same: what did you actually do?' },
      { p: 'Take the wide stance and pump the breath up high into the top of your chest until you are almost too big to move. Speak from up there: “Everyone else panicked. I was the only one prepared to do what had to be done.” Then let something behind you make a sudden noise, and say the same sentence again with the breath fallen out of it. That drop is the whole character.' },
      { p: 'The Captain has cousins. Scaramuccia, the swordsman and adventurer who became the French Scaramouche, mixes the Captain’s swagger with real charm and a gift for escaping.' },
      { h: 'Where you can see him' },
      { list: [
        'The braggart soldier of Roman comedy, in Plautus’s play Miles Gloriosus, his ancestor.',
        'Pistol and Falstaff in Shakespeare’s history plays, both partly his descendants.',
        'Gaston in Beauty and the Beast, who never stops admiring himself.',
        'Gilderoy Lockhart in Harry Potter and the Chamber of Secrets, famous for adventures he never had.',
        'Zapp Brannigan in Futurama, the heroic commander who hides behind his crew.',
      ] },
    ] },

  { id: 'ch-cm-brighella', module: 'commedia', order: 105, reference: true,
    title: 'Brighella',
    attribution: 'Commedia tradition',
    requiredReviewer: 'acting-professional',
    objective: 'Learn Brighella, the first zanni who runs the plot, and how a schemer holds a stage.',
    orientation: 'Brighella is the one who knows how everything works, and he charges for it.',
    reflection: 'Brighella rarely lies. He chooses which part of the truth to tell. When have you watched someone do that well?',
    body: [
      { p: 'Brighella is the first zanni: Arlecchino’s countryman from Bergamo and his opposite. Where Arlecchino blunders, Brighella calculates. He is often an innkeeper, a go-between or the senior servant in Pantalone’s house, and in almost every plot he is the one who invents the scheme: the forged letter, the disguise, the plan to marry the lovers under the old men’s noses.' },
      { p: 'The phrase that unlocks him is master of the servants and servant of the masters. Picture the head waiter in an expensive restaurant: proud of the uniform, aware he worked his way into it, smooth with the important guests, hard on the staff beneath him, and entirely informed about what is happening in the kitchen. He does not own the place. He knows how it actually runs, which is worth more, and every favour he grants has a price he worked out before you asked.' },
      { fig: 'cm-brighella-mask' },
      { h: 'At a glance' },
      { mask: 'brighella' },
      { h: 'Relationships' },
      { list: [
        'Arlecchino: his partner, his tool and his despair. Brighella plans; Arlecchino executes, badly.',
        'The lovers: his clients. He helps them because it pays, and he makes sure it pays.',
        'Pantalone: the master he flatters to his face and fleeces behind his back.',
        'Colombina: the one person he cannot fool, so the two of them either work together or circle each other warily.',
        'Il Capitano: an easy mark. Brighella knows the Captain is hollow and turns the swagger against him.',
      ] },
      { h: 'The first zanni' },
      { p: 'Brighella is the classic first zanni. The two servant jobs are theatrical functions, not measurements of intelligence, and the same mask could play either one in a different play. The first zanni:' },
      { list: ['Advances the plot.', 'Understands status and incentives.', 'Manipulates the other characters.', 'Watches before acting.', 'Conserves his energy.', 'Changes allegiance when it is useful.', 'Uses language deliberately.', 'Rarely panics without first working out an escape.'] },
      { p: 'Try it: fetch a chair from across the room as the first zanni. Look at the chair, look at whoever asked, work out what is in it for you, and only then move, smoothly and no faster than you need to.' },
      { h: 'The physical breakdown' },
      { profile: 'brighella' },
      { fig: 'cm-brighella-stance' },
      { fig: 'cm-brighella-walk' },
      { h: 'Find it' },
      { p: 'Walk the room slowly with your weight low and your eyes moving, as if every person you pass might be worth something. Spot an imaginary coin on the floor and go from slow to instant and back to slow again, as if nothing happened.' },
      { p: 'Stay low and let the breath settle deep and slow, the breath of somebody who is never hurried because he already knows how this ends. Keep it even while you say “I am not asking you to lie. I am asking you to tell the part of the truth that helps us,” and say it warmly enough that it sounds like a favour. Nothing in the voice should rise. That is what makes it frightening.' },
      { h: 'Where you can see him' },
      { list: [
        'Scapin in Molière’s Scapin the Schemer, a servant who runs rings around everyone.',
        'Figaro in The Barber of Seville and The Marriage of Figaro, the fixer who is cleverer than his master.',
        'Iago in Othello, the same gift for framing, turned deadly.',
        'Saul Goodman in Better Call Saul, who always has an angle and a way out.',
        'Ferris Bueller in Ferris Bueller’s Day Off, who talks every adult in town into what he wants.',
      ] },
    ] },

  { id: 'ch-cm-arlecchino', module: 'commedia', order: 106, reference: true,
    title: 'Arlecchino',
    attribution: 'Commedia tradition',
    requiredReviewer: 'acting-professional',
    objective: 'Learn Arlecchino, the hungry second zanni who takes every word literally.',
    orientation: 'The most famous servant in the tradition: always hungry, always moving, and never quite understanding what he was told.',
    reflection: 'Arlecchino wins more often than anyone above him. Why do you think nobody sees him coming?',
    body: [
      { p: 'Arlecchino is the most famous second zanni, the servant who carries out the plans and gets them wrong. He comes from the hills around Bergamo, and he carries the wooden bat that gave slapstick its name. In one plot he serves Pantalone, in the next one of the lovers or the Captain. Whoever employs him, he is thinking about lunch.' },
      { p: 'He is not stupid, and playing him stupid kills him. He simply does not run on the kind of logic that plans three moves ahead. He runs on appetite, instinct and whatever is physically in front of him, which is why he sometimes arrives at a solution a careful schemer would never have found. Brighella thinks and then moves. Arlecchino moves, and the thinking catches up afterwards.' },
      { fig: 'cm-arlecchino-mask' },
      { h: 'At a glance' },
      { mask: 'arlecchino' },
      { h: 'Relationships' },
      { list: [
        'Brighella: his partner and his boss. Brighella makes the plan; Arlecchino carries it out and ruins it.',
        'Pantalone and the other masters: whoever pays him, feeds him badly and beats him when things go wrong.',
        'Colombina: his sweetheart, and much smarter than he is. He adores her, and she keeps him out of trouble, mostly.',
        'Pedrolino: his rival for Colombina, who loves her sincerely and usually loses her to Arlecchino.',
        'Il Capitano: the bully he is terrified of, until he discovers the Captain is even more frightened than he is.',
      ] },
      { h: 'The second zanni' },
      { p: 'Arlecchino is the classic second zanni, and Truffaldino after him plays the same function. Like the first zanni, it is a job in the plot rather than a measure of intelligence. The second zanni:' },
      { list: ['Disrupts the plot.', 'Misunderstands instructions.', 'Reacts before thinking.', 'Follows his appetite.', 'Spends far more energy than any task needs.', 'Creates complications by trying to obey.', 'Sometimes discovers the solution by accident.'] },
      { p: 'Try it: fetch a chair from across the room as the second zanni. Be moving before the instruction has finished, arrive at the wrong chair, bring it back proudly, and only then realise what you were asked.' },
      { h: 'The frightened zanni run' },
      { p: 'Contemporary commedia training commonly teaches a frightened run for the second zanni. It is a convention of modern training, built in the spirit of the tradition, not a movement documented beat by beat from the 1500s. It will also look familiar, because the same idea runs through cartoon chases, where the legs spin in place before the body shoots away. That resemblance shows how far the principle travelled. It does not prove that one historical movement was copied directly.' },
      { steps: [
        'Zanni notices the danger.',
        'His eyes, and the mask, lock onto it.',
        'His torso recoils while his feet stay planted.',
        'His legs start running before the body travels anywhere.',
        'Arms and legs accelerate frantically.',
        'For a moment the torso seems to be left behind.',
        'The whole body finally catches up with the feet and shoots away.',
        'He may pick the wrong direction, collide with something, or come back because he forgot something important.',
      ] },
      { p: 'Movement principle: his feet understand the danger before the rest of him does.' },
      { h: 'Try the run safely' },
      { p: 'Practise it slowly and on the spot first: the look, the recoil, the running feet in place, the arms joining in. Keep the steps small and light and work on a clear floor that will not slip. Only when each beat is clean should you let the body travel, and even then a few steps is plenty. The joke lives in the order of events, not in the speed.' },
      { h: 'The physical breakdown' },
      { profile: 'arlecchino' },
      { fig: 'cm-arlecchino-stance' },
      { fig: 'cm-arlecchino-walk' },
      { h: 'Find it' },
      { p: 'Bend your knees a little, lift your heels, and cross the room in quick short steps. Stop dead. Look at something with just your head. Now let your stomach remind you it is empty, and see how far across the room it pulls you. Keep it light and inside what your body allows; the acrobatics came later, with training.' },
      { p: 'Now let the breath ride high and quick, catching on every new thing in the room. Let it break into sound before it turns into any words at all. Then explain yourself: “I did exactly what you told me. You said watch the door, so I watched the door.” Stop moving the instant you have to think, and start again the instant you do not. The mouth and the feet run on the same engine.' },
      { h: 'Where you can see him' },
      { list: [
        'Harlequin, his direct descendant, in the English pantomime.',
        'Truffaldino in Carlo Goldoni’s The Servant of Two Masters, a hungry servant working two jobs at once and wrecking both.',
        'Mr. Bean, who solves every problem with total confidence and none of the right information.',
        'Kramer in Seinfeld, bursting through the door and following every impulse the moment it arrives.',
        'Buddy in Elf, who takes every word literally and meets the world with his whole body.',
      ] },
    ] },

  { id: 'ch-cm-colombina', module: 'commedia', order: 107, reference: true,
    title: 'Colombina',
    attribution: 'Commedia tradition',
    requiredReviewer: 'acting-professional',
    objective: 'Learn Colombina, the servetta who sees through everyone, and how to play perception without contempt.',
    orientation: 'Colombina has no power at all, and she is the only person on stage nobody can fool.',
    reflection: 'Colombina’s status comes from attention, not rank. Who in your life holds a room that way?',
    body: [
      { p: 'Colombina is the servetta, the maidservant, also called Columbina and in some companies Arlecchina. She is often in service to the young woman of the house, and sometimes to Pantalone himself. Where the lovers sigh and the old men bluster, she gets things done, and she is the character most likely to turn and tell the audience exactly what she thinks of them all. Play her with affection, not contempt: she likes these people. She just sees straight through them.' },
      { fig: 'cm-colombina-mask' },
      { h: 'At a glance' },
      { mask: 'colombina' },
      { h: 'Relationships' },
      { list: [
        'The young woman she serves: her charge, and often her friend. Colombina solves the love problems her mistress can only describe.',
        'Arlecchino: her sweetheart. She knows exactly what he is and loves him anyway.',
        'Pedrolino: the one who loves her sincerely. She is kind to him and seldom chooses him.',
        'Pantalone and Il Capitano: the old man who chases her and the braggart who boasts at her. She deflates them both.',
        'Brighella: the only other person who sees the whole game. They are allies when it suits them.',
      ] },
      { h: 'The physical breakdown' },
      { profile: 'colombina' },
      { fig: 'cm-colombina-stance' },
      { fig: 'cm-colombina-walk' },
      { h: 'Find it' },
      { p: 'Stand with your weight even and your hands on your hips. Listen to an imaginary master explain a terrible plan. Do not roll your eyes or comment. Just hold a level look, a beat too long, and then say one word: “No.”' },
      { p: 'Keep the breath low and unhurried, the breath of the only person present who is not panicking. Say “You can keep explaining why your plan will work, or you can listen while I tell you why it will not,” at a completely ordinary volume. Then stop, and let the silence finish the job. She is the one character who can turn and say it straight to the audience, so try it that way too.' },
      { h: 'Where you can see her' },
      { list: [
        'Dorine in Molière’s Tartuffe, on the Scenes shelf, who tells her master’s household the truth nobody else will.',
        'Maria in Twelfth Night, who runs the plot against Malvolio.',
        'Suzanne in The Marriage of Figaro, smarter than every man in the house.',
        'Columbine, her descendant in the English pantomime, forever pursued by Harlequin and Pierrot.',
      ] },
    ] },

  { id: 'ch-cm-pulcinella', module: 'commedia', order: 108, reference: true,
    title: 'Pulcinella',
    attribution: 'Commedia tradition',
    requiredReviewer: 'acting-professional',
    objective: 'Learn Pulcinella, the contradictory survivor from Naples.',
    orientation: 'Pulcinella is everything at once: clever and stupid, cruel and pitiful. He never resolves it. He survives it.',
    reflection: 'Both halves of Pulcinella’s line are sincere. Can you say it without choosing between them?',
    body: [
      { p: 'Pulcinella is the great mask of Naples. He has no fixed job: in one play he is a servant, in the next a baker, a thief, a husband or a fool, and in every one of them he is in trouble and talking his way out of it. He never resolves his contradictions. He lives inside them.' },
      { fig: 'cm-pulcinella-mask' },
      { h: 'At a glance' },
      { mask: 'pulcinella' },
      { h: 'Relationships' },
      { list: [
        'Everyone: an argument waiting to happen. Pulcinella quarrels with masters, servants, wives and officials alike.',
        'Arlecchino: his northern counterpart. Where Arlecchino is quick and light, Pulcinella is heavier, slyer and full of self-pity.',
        'Masters and officials: he fears them, cheats them, and outlasts them.',
      ] },
      { h: 'The physical breakdown' },
      { profile: 'pulcinella' },
      { fig: 'cm-pulcinella-stance' },
      { fig: 'cm-pulcinella-walk' },
      { h: 'Find it' },
      { p: 'Let your spine round and your belly soften forward, keeping it comfortable. Shuffle a few steps. Then let your head peck toward something interesting, two or three quick jerks, and scurry after it before settling back into the slump.' },
      { p: 'Let the breath push up high and thin, in front of the beak rather than down in the chest. Complain about something very small, at length, and let the pitch climb every time you are contradicted. Then defend yourself: “Of course I am innocent. And if I did do it, you should be thanking me.” The voice should sound like it is already arguing before anyone has disagreed.' },
      { h: 'Where you can see him' },
      { list: [
        'Mr Punch in Punch and Judy, his English descendant, who kept the stick, the squawk and the talent for escaping consequences.',
        'Sancho Panza in Don Quixote, a servant ruled by his stomach and his common sense.',
        'Falstaff, who shares his appetite and his gift for talking his way out of anything.',
        'George Costanza in Seinfeld, forever scheming, complaining and dodging the consequences.',
      ] },
    ] },

  { id: 'ch-cm-pedrolino', module: 'commedia', order: 109, reference: true,
    title: 'Pedrolino',
    attribution: 'Commedia tradition',
    requiredReviewer: 'acting-professional',
    objective: 'Learn Pedrolino, the honest servant, and the hardest thing in comedy to play: sincerity.',
    orientation: 'Pedrolino cannot scheme because he cannot pretend. Everything he feels is on his face.',
    reflection: 'Pedrolino does not fight for status at all. What happens to an audience when a character stops fighting?',
    body: [
      { p: 'Pedrolino is a gentle, trusting servant. In a world of liars and schemers he is the one character who says exactly what he means, and it rarely goes well for him. He is blamed for the others’ tricks, beaten for their mistakes, and in love with someone who prefers someone else. In France he became Pierrot, the sad clown in white, looking at the moon.' },
      { fig: 'cm-pedrolino-mask' },
      { h: 'At a glance' },
      { mask: 'pedrolino' },
      { h: 'Relationships' },
      { list: [
        'Colombina: the one he loves. She is fond of him and usually chooses Arlecchino.',
        'Arlecchino: the rival who wins, and whom Pedrolino cannot quite bring himself to hate.',
        'Pantalone and the masters: the people he serves faithfully, who blame him when things go wrong.',
        'Brighella: the schemer who uses Pedrolino’s honesty as a tool, or makes him the scapegoat.',
      ] },
      { h: 'The physical breakdown' },
      { profile: 'pedrolino' },
      { fig: 'cm-pedrolino-stance' },
      { fig: 'cm-pedrolino-walk' },
      { h: 'Find it' },
      { p: 'Stand with your arms loose and your chest open. Imagine someone you care about has just told you they are leaving with someone else. Do not act sad. Let it land, completely, and let the audience watch it land.' },
      { p: 'Stay open and let the breath be visible, the way it is in somebody who is not managing their face. Take far longer than is comfortable. Then say “I know you might not feel the same. I just did not want to pretend I do not care,” simply, with no colour added, and let the pause afterwards do the rest. He is the one who never protects himself, and the breath is where you can see it first.' },
      { h: 'Where you can see him' },
      { list: [
        'Pierrot, his French descendant, the white-faced clown of pantomime.',
        'The gentle, hopeful side of the Tramp in Charlie Chaplin’s films.',
        'Paddington, whose sincerity survives every disaster he causes.',
        'Forrest Gump, who says exactly what he means in a world that rarely does.',
      ] },
    ] },

  { id: 'ch-cm-lazzi', module: 'commedia', order: 9,
    title: 'Lazzi',
    attribution: 'Commedia tradition',
    requiredReviewer: 'acting-professional',
    objective: 'Understand lazzi, the rehearsed comic routines of commedia, and why they have to be exact.',
    orientation: 'The most spontaneous-looking moments in commedia were the most rehearsed.',
    reflection: 'What is the smallest physical problem you could build a whole lazzo around?',
    body: [
      { p: 'A lazzo, plural lazzi, is a prepared piece of comic business that an actor could drop into a performance whenever the scene called for it. Each character had their own. Arlecchino catches a fly buzzing around his head, stalks it, snatches it out of the air and eats it with enormous satisfaction. A servant tries to carry two trays at once, keep two lies going at once, or serve two masters at once. A starving servant eats an imaginary meal, course by course. Some actors became famous for particular lazzi, and audiences came expecting to see them.' },
      { p: 'Lazzi were not the only thing an actor carried. Alongside them came the generici, the verbal stock: speeches, declarations, insults and laments written to fit almost any comedy. The two overlap at the edges, and the difference is worth holding on to. The generici gave an actor something to say in any situation. The lazzi gave them something to do. Catalogues of lazzi survive, sorted by type and running to hundreds of entries, which is the clearest evidence there is that none of this was made up on the spot. The generici survive too. In 1607 one actor published his Captain’s entire stock, a whole book of boasts, challenges and declarations of valour for a character who never fought anyone.' },
      { p: 'The catalogues are also cruder than most modern accounts of commedia admit. Alongside the lazzi of food and of fear sit a great many about the body and what it does, and Il Dottore attracts more than his share of them. That is not an accident and it is not decoration. He is the character whose entire identity is Latin, learning and being the cleverest person in the room, which makes him the funniest possible person for a body to betray. The rule underneath every one of these routines is the one the Slapstick chapter already states: what gets hurt is dignity. The cruder the business, the higher the dignity it is aimed at.' },
      { p: 'A lazzo usually stops the plot instead of moving it forward. That is its job: a breath of pure play in the middle of the intrigue before the story picks up again. The best ones grow straight out of what the character wants. Arlecchino’s fly is funny because Arlecchino is always hungry. Give the same routine to Pantalone and it becomes a different joke about a different man.' },
      { p: 'Here is the part actors forget: lazzi look improvised and are the opposite. A lazzo is built beat by beat and rehearsed until it is exact, because comedy lives in timing, and a physical joke that is a little bit vague is not a little bit funny. It is not funny at all. The improvisation in commedia happened around the lazzi. The lazzi themselves were set.' },
      { h: 'Build one' },
      { list: [
        'Choose a character and the want that drives them.',
        'Choose a small problem that gets in the way: a fly, a stuck door, a hat that will not stay on, a letter that must stay hidden.',
        'Find the first attempt, the complication, the escalation and the payoff. Four beats is plenty.',
        'Run it slowly until every beat is clear, then bring it up to speed. Keep one clear look to the audience somewhere in it.',
        'Now run it the same way five times. If it keeps changing, it is not a lazzo yet.',
      ] },
      { p: 'Work at a scale your body is comfortable with. The classic lazzi included falls and acrobatics that took years of training. Yours need none of that to be good.' },
    ] },

  // ── Module 3: The Physical Character (written 2026-09-23) ──────
  // The vocabulary the commedia chapters already use, taught as
  // transferable craft: centre, what leads, weight, tempo, space, walk,
  // animal and signature gesture. Every lesson carries a safety line,
  // because this is the first module that asks a learner to move.
  { id: 'ch-ph-own', module: 'physical', order: 1,
    title: 'Your Own Body First',
    attribution: 'Speechcraft original',
    requiredReviewer: 'acting-professional',
    objective: 'Notice your own physical habits, so that you know what you are taking off before you put anyone else on.',
    orientation: 'You already have a walk, a centre and a set of gestures. You simply stopped noticing them years ago.',
    reflection: 'Which of your own habits would be hardest to leave behind for a role, and why that one?',
    body: [
      { p: 'You arrive with a body that already has opinions. It has a preferred way of standing, a speed it walks at when nothing is urgent, a hand it gestures with, a place it holds tension, and a distance it likes to keep from other people. None of that is neutral. It is a character, and it is the one you have been playing your whole life.' },
      { p: 'This matters for a practical reason. Unexamined habits leak. If your weight always sits back on your heels, every person you play will stand back on their heels, and you will call that your instrument when it is really one option out of many that you never noticed you were choosing.' },
      { h: 'Try it: the inventory' },
      { steps: [
        'Stand and do nothing for thirty seconds. Do not correct anything. Just find out what your body does when nobody has asked it to do something.',
        'Where is your weight: forward over the toes, back on the heels, even, or more on one side?',
        'What are your hands doing? Where do they go when you stop thinking about them?',
        'Walk the length of the room at your ordinary speed. What part of you arrives first?',
        'Now stop, and notice where you hold tension. Jaw, shoulders, lower back, hands. Everyone has somewhere.',
        'Write down four things. Those are your defaults, and from now on they are choices.',
      ] },
      { p: 'Do this again in a week and some of it will have changed, because a body on a bad night is a different instrument from a body on a good one. That is worth knowing too. Actors who cannot tell the difference between their own tiredness and a character choice end up playing tiredness in everything.' },
      { p: 'Neutral does not mean blank, and nobody is asking you to erase yourself. Neutral means knowing your own settings well enough to leave them deliberately. Everything in this module is a way of changing one of them on purpose.' },
      { p: 'Work gently and inside what your body allows today. Nothing in this module needs strain, pain, deep crouches or falls to be useful, and if something hurts, it is the wrong version of the exercise.' },
    ] },

  { id: 'ch-ph-leads', module: 'physical', order: 2,
    title: 'Where the Body Leads',
    attribution: 'Michael Chekhov',
    requiredReviewer: 'acting-professional',
    objective: 'Find the centre a person moves from, and the part of them that enters a room first.',
    orientation: 'Watch anybody cross a room and something goes first. It is almost never the feet.',
    reflection: 'Pick somebody you know well. What part of them arrives before the rest, and what does that tell you about what they want?',
    body: [
      { p: 'Imagine the energy a person moves with comes from one place in their body, and that everything else follows it. That place is their centre. A person centred in the chest looks open, or proud, or exposed. A person centred low in the belly looks settled, appetitive, hard to move. A person centred in the head looks like they are thinking their way across the room and have left their body to manage on its own.' },
      { p: 'Close to that idea, and easier to play, is what leads. The centre is where the movement comes from. What leads is the part that gets there first. They are often the same and they do not have to be. Somebody can be centred in the belly and still lead with the nose, which is exactly what makes them look greedy rather than grounded.' },
      { h: 'You have already met this' },
      { p: 'Every commedia breakdown in this course lists a centre and a what-leads, because those two answers are most of what makes each mask look different from the others. Pantalone is divided between a guarded pelvis and a searching nose, and that contradiction is the whole man. Il Dottore is belly and chest, leading with the belly, so his opinions arrive before he does. Arlecchino sits low in the belly where the hunger lives, with the feet beneath it. Pedrolino is the heart and the open chest, which is why nothing he feels can be hidden.' },
      { h: 'Try it: five centres' },
      { steps: [
        'Walk the room normally, and notice your own centre from the last lesson.',
        'Now imagine a small warm weight behind the breastbone, and let it pull you across the room. Walk. Then speak one sentence from there.',
        'Move the weight down into the belly. Walk again. Speak the same sentence.',
        'Move it up into the forehead. Walk. Speak.',
        'Put it in the pelvis. Walk. Speak.',
        'Finally, keep the weight in the belly but let your nose lead. Walk. Speak. Notice that you now look like you want something.',
      ] },
      { p: 'The sentence should change every time without you deciding to change it. That is the point of the exercise, and it is the same principle the mask work rests on: alter the shape and the voice follows, because a body arranged differently cannot breathe the same way.' },
      { p: 'Work at a scale that is comfortable, and keep the weight imaginary. None of this asks you to hold a posture that strains.' },
    ] },

  { id: 'ch-ph-imaginary', module: 'physical', order: 3,
    title: 'The Imaginary Body',
    attribution: 'Michael Chekhov',
    requiredReviewer: 'acting-professional',
    objective: 'Wear a body that is not yours, from the inside, without changing your actual one.',
    orientation: 'You cannot lengthen your arms or widen your back. You can change the body you imagine yourself standing inside.',
    reflection: 'Which imaginary body felt least like acting and most like simply being somebody else?',
    body: [
      { p: 'Here is a tool that does more work than it has any right to. Picture a body that is not yours occupying the same space you occupy. Taller, or wider, or heavier through the shoulders, or thin and high and brittle. Do not act it. Step into it, as though putting on a coat, and let your real body do whatever it does in response.' },
      { p: 'What makes this useful is that it changes many things at once. A person who decides to play somebody heavy has to remember to move slowly, take up room, breathe low and speak deliberately, and they will drop two of those the moment they have a line to think about. A person standing inside an imagined heavy body gets all four for free, and keeps them while they think about something else.' },
      { h: 'Try it: three bodies' },
      { steps: [
        'Stand in neutral. Imagine a body around you that is a head taller than you and broader through the back. Take one breath inside it. Walk four steps. Say your own name.',
        'Shake it off. Now imagine a body that is small, light and quick, with the weight high in the chest. Breathe. Walk. Say your name.',
        'Shake it off. Now one that is heavy and low, wide through the hips, slow to start and slow to stop. Breathe. Walk. Say your name.',
        'Come back to neutral and notice how strongly your own body wants to return to it.',
      ] },
      { p: 'Three things will have changed without you managing any of them: how much room you took, how long your sentences wanted to be, and where your voice sat. That is the tool working. It gives you a whole physical logic from one image, which is far more reliable than a list of adjustments you have to hold in mind.' },
      { p: 'The trap is going too far and losing yourself in the image. The imagined body is a way in, not a performance. Once it has changed how you move, stop thinking about it and get on with what the character wants. If an audience can see you concentrating on your own physicality, the tool has stopped helping.' },
      { p: 'Keep every imagined body inside what your real one can comfortably do. You are not required to strain, crouch, hunch or hold anything that hurts, and nothing here needs a shape your body objects to.' },
    ] },

  { id: 'ch-ph-weight', module: 'physical', order: 4,
    title: 'Weight, Tempo and Space',
    attribution: 'Rudolf Laban',
    requiredReviewer: 'acting-professional',
    objective: 'Use three dials that change a person: how heavy they are, how fast, and how much room they take.',
    orientation: 'Almost every physical difference between two people can be described with three settings.',
    reflection: 'Which of the three dials do you habitually leave in one position, whatever you are playing?',
    body: [
      { p: 'Three qualities do most of the work, and the useful thing about them is that they are dials rather than switches. You can turn each one a little rather than all the way, which is how you get a person instead of a caricature.' },
      { list: [
        'Weight: light or strong. Does this person move as if gravity barely applies to them, or as if every gesture has force behind it? Light is not weak, and strong is not loud. A quiet threat is played strong.',
        'Tempo: quick or sustained. Does movement arrive in sudden bursts, or unfold and keep going? Tempo is separate from speed of speech, and the mismatch between them is often the most interesting thing about somebody.',
        'Space: direct or indirect. Does this person go straight at what they want, or approach it around the side? Direct is a straight line through a room. Indirect wanders, circles and arrives eventually.',
      ] },
      { p: 'A fourth question sits alongside them and belongs to status rather than movement: how much room does this person believe they are entitled to? Somebody who takes up space they have not earned is comic. Somebody who takes up less than they are owed is somebody you worry about.' },
      { h: 'Try it: one line, eight ways' },
      { p: 'Take any ordinary sentence. “I did not think you would come.” Play it light and quick and direct. Then light, quick and indirect. Work through every combination of the three dials, eight in total, and keep the words identical. Somewhere in those eight is a person you have not played before.' },
      { h: 'Reading the masks with it' },
      { p: 'The dials describe the commedia characters efficiently, which is a good test of any vocabulary. Arlecchino is light, quick and indirect: he goes at everything sideways and at speed. Il Dottore is strong, sustained and indirect, which is why an explanation from him never arrives anywhere. Colombina is light, quick and direct, the only one in the company who goes straight at the problem. Il Capitano is strong, sustained and direct, right up to the moment he is frightened, when all three settings flip at once.' },
      { p: 'Work within your own comfortable range. Strong does not mean forcing, and quick does not mean rushing at something and hurting yourself.' },
    ] },

  { id: 'ch-ph-walk', module: 'physical', order: 5,
    title: 'The Walk',
    attribution: 'Movement training tradition',
    requiredReviewer: 'acting-professional',
    objective: 'Build a walk by changing one thing at a time, and find the rhythm underneath it.',
    orientation: 'A walk is not a pose that travels. It is a rhythm, and rhythm is the part an audience reads from the back of the room.',
    reflection: 'When you built your walk, which single change did the most work?',
    body: [
      { p: 'A walk is the most public thing a character has. It arrives before the face is readable and before a word is spoken, and an audience has usually decided a great deal about somebody by the time they have crossed a stage. That is why it is worth building deliberately rather than hoping one turns up.' },
      { p: 'Build it by changing one thing at a time. Change everything at once and you get a costume that falls off the moment you have to think about a line. Change one variable, walk with it until it stops feeling like a decision, then add the next.' },
      { h: 'The variables' },
      { list: [
        'Step size. Long strides, or small quick steps that cover no ground.',
        'Speed, and whether it is constant or arrives in bursts.',
        'What leads, from the last lessons. The walk follows whatever goes first.',
        'Where the weight falls: heels, toes, or rolling through the whole foot.',
        'What the arms do, which is usually whatever you forgot to decide.',
        'The pathway. Straight across the room, or around the edge of it.',
        'And the one most actors miss: what happens when the walk stops. Starting and stopping tell an audience more than the travelling does.',
      ] },
      { h: 'Try it: build one' },
      { steps: [
        'Walk normally. Now change one thing only: take smaller steps. Keep going until it is no longer an effort to remember.',
        'Add a second change: let the chest lead. Walk again.',
        'Add a third: put the weight back on the heels.',
        'Now stop suddenly, as though somebody said your name. Notice what the rest of your body does when the feet stop.',
        'Walk again and let a thought arrive while you are moving. Does this person stop to think, or think while walking? That single answer is most of a character.',
      ] },
      { p: 'The commedia walks in this course are all built this way, which is why each one can be described in a sentence. Pantalone travels in darts, still then suddenly quick, and the nose finds the target before the feet move. Brighella is slow and even until a single fast step, then slow again as though nothing happened. Arlecchino goes feet first and lets the thinking catch up. Each is one rhythm, chosen and repeatable.' },
      { p: 'Keep the steps small and light, work on a floor that will not slip, and leave out anything that strains a knee, hip or back. A walk does not need to be extreme to read.' },
    ] },

  { id: 'ch-ph-animal', module: 'physical', order: 6,
    title: 'Animal Work',
    attribution: 'Movement training tradition',
    requiredReviewer: 'acting-professional',
    objective: 'Watch an animal closely, then let a little of it into a person.',
    orientation: 'The oldest trick in physical character work, and the easiest one to overdo.',
    reflection: 'How little of the animal could you keep and still feel it changing you?',
    body: [
      { p: 'Choose an animal and watch it properly, for longer than is comfortable. Not a cartoon of it and not your memory of it: the real thing, in a video if you have no other way. Watch how it holds its weight when nothing is happening. Watch what it does with its head. Watch how it starts moving, and how it stops, and what it does when it is startled and when it wants something.' },
      { p: 'Then take almost none of it. The finished result should be a person, with perhaps one percent of the animal left in the rhythm, the stillness or the way the head turns. An audience should never think “that is a bird.” They should think there is something about that man that makes them uneasy, without being able to say why.' },
      { h: 'Why it works' },
      { p: 'It works because animals do not hide anything. A cat wanting something is entirely a cat wanting something, with no social layer over it, and watching that reminds an actor what pure want looks like in a body. It is also a shortcut past your own defaults, which is why it survives in training everywhere: it hands you rhythms and stillnesses you would never have chosen from your own habits.' },
      { h: 'Try it' },
      { steps: [
        'Choose your animal and watch it for five minutes without doing anything else.',
        'On your feet, go too far first. Let yourself be entirely the animal, at whatever scale your body is comfortable with, standing upright. This is private work and it is meant to be ridiculous.',
        'Now take it down by half. Then half again. Then half again.',
        'Stop when a person walks into the room who is not you, and who carries something of the animal in how they wait, how they turn and how they start moving.',
        'Say a line as that person. Then take the animal out entirely and say it again, and notice what you lost.',
      ] },
      { p: 'This is the commit-and-sculpt shape from the commedia module applied to one tool: go too far on purpose, then remove until only the mechanism is left. Several masks in this course carry an animal in their sensory image for exactly this reason, and Arlecchino is the clearest, described as somewhere between a cat and a monkey.' },
      { p: 'Two cautions. Keep all of it upright and inside your comfortable range: nothing here requires crawling, jumping or weight on the hands. And never use this to build a person out of a disability, an illness or an ethnicity. The animal is a source of rhythm and attention, and it stops there.' },
    ] },

  { id: 'ch-ph-gesture', module: 'physical', order: 7,
    title: 'The Signature Gesture',
    attribution: 'Michael Chekhov',
    requiredReviewer: 'acting-professional',
    objective: 'Find one gesture that holds what a character wants, and learn how rarely to use it.',
    orientation: 'One movement, made with the whole body, can carry an entire want. Used twice in a scene it becomes a tic.',
    reflection: 'What is the gesture for somebody who wants to be forgiven but will not ask?',
    body: [
      { p: 'Take what a character wants and turn it into a single physical action made with the whole body rather than the hands. Somebody who wants to hold on to everything might close and pull inward. Somebody who wants to be seen might open and lift. Somebody who wants to push the world away might press outward and down. The gesture is not mime and it is not illustration. It is the want, made physical, at full size.' },
      { p: 'You rehearse it large and privately, several times, until the shape is in the body and carries a feeling with it. Then you put it away. It almost never appears on stage at full size. What appears is the residue: the pull inward reduced to a hand closing on a sleeve, the lift reduced to a chin. The audience never sees the gesture. They see everything it left behind.' },
      { h: 'Try it' },
      { steps: [
        'Pick a character, your own or one of the masks, and say in one sentence what they want.',
        'Find a whole-body movement that is that want. Make it big and repeat it six times, letting the breath go with it.',
        'Notice the feeling it produces. Do not manufacture the feeling. Let the shape do it.',
        'Now shrink the gesture until it is barely visible, and keep the same breath.',
        'Say a line while the small version is happening somewhere in your body.',
      ] },
      { h: 'Where you have already seen it' },
      { p: 'Every commedia breakdown carries a signature action, and they are all built this way. Pantalone hears money, locates it with his nose, checks his own purse, advances in tiny steps, reaches, is threatened, contracts around both, and retreats still watching. That is a want turned into a sequence you can repeat exactly. Il Capitano grows while safe and collapses the instant he is not, which is one gesture and its opposite.' },
      { p: 'The discipline is in how rarely you spend it. A signature gesture that shows up in every scene stops meaning anything and becomes a mannerism the audience waits for. Once in a performance, at the moment it costs the character most, is usually right.' },
      { p: 'Keep the movement inside a comfortable range and let the size come from commitment rather than strain.' },
    ] },

  // ── Lessons first written for Acting's Building a Character module ──
  // (owner order 2026-09-22: the course takes them over). Acting keeps its
  // own copies until this course is built; then Acting's module comes
  // out, and each record here takes over the Acting id named in
  // `movesFrom`, so learners keep their progress. Copied word for word
  // except the sentences built on the retired "two roads" framing.
  { id: 'ch-inside', module: 'piece', order: 1, movesFrom: 'ac-inside',
    title: 'Through Feeling',
    attribution: 'Eric Bogosian',
    requiredReviewer: 'acting-professional',
    objective: 'Understand where the characters you invent actually come from, and start the search there.',
    orientation: 'Inventing a person does not start at a desk. It starts with everything you have ever absorbed.',
    reflection: 'Who has spoken up in you before, unprompted? And can you name where you absorbed them from?',
    body: [
      { p: 'Start with how a person learns to be anything. A doctor behaves like a doctor because doctors have been shown behaving that way. A truck driver talks like a truck driver because that is how truck drivers talk, and everyone, including the truck driver, learned it from somewhere. Behavior is not born in us. It is absorbed, from every person we have ever watched.' },
      { p: 'And you have watched more than the people in your life. You were never a cowboy. You never robbed a bank. But you know exactly how a cowboy leans in a doorway and exactly how a bank robber tells a room to stay calm, because the movies and television have been feeding you cowboys and bank robbers since before you could read. To the part of you that produces behavior, a remembered performance is as real as a remembered person. It does not sort its library into firsthand and secondhand. It just shelves everything.' },
      { p: 'Which means the cast is already in the building. A lifetime of absorbed people: met, overheard, watched on screens. So when inventing a character means looking inside instead of out, it is not mysticism. The inside IS the outside, collected. Improvise out loud with no plan, play the recording back, and one of them will step forward, someone you never planned, saying things you did not write. That is not invention from nothing. That is the library lending.' },
      { p: 'So forget building a character the way you would build a shelf. There is no finishing it. You are pulling somebody out of a collection you spent your whole life making, and you will know it is working when they start saying things you did not plan.' },
    ] },

  { id: 'ch-room', module: 'piece', order: 2, movesFrom: 'ac-room',
    title: 'The Empty Room',
    attribution: 'Eric Bogosian',
    requiredReviewer: 'acting-professional',
    objective: 'Set up a space where you can improvise without watching yourself.',
    orientation: 'This part takes no talent at all. It takes a door that closes.',
    reflection: 'Where and when could you do this without being overheard? Name the room and the hour.',
    body: [
      { p: 'The setup could not be plainer. A recorder. A room where nobody can hear you. Enough floor to move around on. Say the date, say what you are working on, and go.' },
      { p: 'The privacy is the whole machine. The second some part of you starts wondering how this sounds to another person, you are performing instead of finding, and whatever was about to show up will not. Self-consciousness kills this work faster than anything else. So no audience, no roommate down the hall, no phone face up on the table.' },
      { p: 'And record everything. Keep all of it. This method was invented on cassette tape, when every reel cost money and had to live in a shoebox, so you had to decide what mattered while you were still making it. You do not. Your phone holds more hours than you will ever fill. Let it run, make the mess, and save the judging for later. Later is when judging is useful.' },
      { p: 'One honest note: Speechcraft cannot do this part with you. The app does not record and does not listen. This is you, a phone, a shut door, and half an hour nobody needs you for. Bring back whatever turns out to be worth keeping.' },
    ] },

  { id: 'ch-waysin', module: 'piece', order: 3, movesFrom: 'ac-waysin',
    title: 'Three Ways In',
    attribution: 'Eric Bogosian',
    requiredReviewer: 'acting-professional',
    objective: 'Start an improvisation from a fragment, a physical detail or a vocal stance.',
    orientation: 'You do not need a whole character to start. You need one piece. The rest follows the piece.',
    reflection: 'Write down three fragments of speech you overheard this week. Which one has a person standing behind it?',
    body: [
      { p: 'Way one: a scrap of overheard speech. Keep your ears open all week. People hand you material constantly: arguments through car windows, one side of a phone call, a guy narrating his grievance to a whole train. Take one scrap and repeat it out loud until an attitude wakes up inside it. One word can be enough. Say “sir” over and over with a little acid in it and watch a whole officious clerk assemble himself around the word.' },
      { p: 'Way two: a physical detail. How somebody lights a cigarette. How they hold a bottle, or will not put down their bag, or cross a room like the floor owes them money. Commit to one honest gesture and the rest of the body tends to show up around it.' },
      { p: 'Way three, and the strongest: a vocal stance. This is not doing an impression. An impression is hollow. This is letting a way of speaking steer the improv from the inside. Take something you know by heart and say it in a voice that is nothing like yours. Listen to what the voice does to the words. It will make choices you would never have made on purpose.' },
      { p: 'And a fourth door, since it is standing open: some people are already performing. A teacher in front of a class. A preacher in the pulpit. A gym instructor counting reps. A man yelling at traffic. Walk in through any of them.' },
    ] },

  { id: 'ch-everyday', module: 'piece', order: 4,
    attribution: 'Eric Bogosian',
    requiredReviewer: 'acting-professional',
    title: 'Everyday Performances',
    objective: 'Collect the performances happening around you every day, and use them to start an improvisation.',
    orientation: 'A teacher in front of a class is already performing. So is the preacher, the trainer, and the man shouting at traffic.',
    reflection: 'Which everyday performance do you watch most often, and what does that person want from the people listening?',
    body: [
      { p: 'Behavior in front of other people is always performance, whoever the audience happens to be. A teacher addressing a class is performing. So is a preacher, a trainer running a session, a tour guide, a manager opening a meeting, a vendor working a queue, a stranger holding forth at a bus stop. None of them would call it acting. Every one of them is shaping how they come across, out loud, in public.' },
      { p: 'Those situations are gifts, because the form does half the work for you. The person has a reason to talk, an audience that cannot easily leave, a job to get done, and a public manner they have built for exactly this. Start an improvisation inside one of them and you are not staring at an empty room. You are already somebody, speaking to somebody, about something, with something to lose.' },
      { p: 'So collect them. When you catch yourself watching one, note what it was: the situation, who was talking, who had to listen, and what the speaker wanted out of it. A sermon. The safety briefing on a plane. A coach at half time. A sales pitch. A toast that goes on too long. An announcement nobody can hear. A lecture from somebody who has read one article.' },
      { h: 'Try it' },
      { steps: [
        'Pick a situation from your list and stand up in the empty room.',
        'Take the public manner first: how does somebody doing this job sound, and how do they stand?',
        'Start talking, in that manner, to a room that has to listen.',
        'Let the person underneath leak through the manner. What do they actually want, and what are they covering?',
        'Run it three times, each with a different want underneath, and keep the one that surprised you.',
      ] },
      { p: 'The manner gets you in the door. What is underneath it is the difference between an impression and a character.' },
    ] },

  { id: 'ch-noaim', module: 'piece', order: 5, movesFrom: 'ac-noaim',
    title: 'Do Not Aim',
    attribution: 'Eric Bogosian',
    requiredReviewer: 'acting-professional',
    objective: 'Improvise without steering toward a result.',
    orientation: 'The fastest way to ruin this is to try to make it good while it is still coming out.',
    reflection: 'Play back something you improvised. Where can you hear yourself trying to be good?',
    body: [
      { p: 'When you improvise, aim at nothing. Do not try to be funny. Do not try to be moving. Do not try to be anything. Become the person, get into the situation, and let whatever happens happen. Your entire job is to cut loose and let the character talk.' },
      { p: 'The finished piece is not your concern right now. The character rambles? Let them ramble. Repeats themselves? People repeat themselves. Makes no sense? Fine. Judging while you create shuts the whole factory down, and creating is the only thing on the schedule today.' },
      { p: 'And expect waste. Plan on it. Throw everything at the wall, because you cannot know in advance what sticks. Three good minutes come out of hours of tape. Hours. That is not you failing, that is the arithmetic of the method, and knowing the arithmetic going in is what gets you through the long stretch where all of it sounds like nothing.' },
    ] },

  { id: 'ch-transcribe', module: 'piece', order: 6, movesFrom: 'ac-transcribe',
    title: 'Listen, Transcribe, Go Again',
    attribution: 'Eric Bogosian',
    requiredReviewer: 'acting-professional',
    objective: 'Turn raw improvisation into material through a loop you repeat.',
    orientation: 'The recording is not the work. The recording is the dirt you sift the work out of.',
    reflection: 'Read your oldest transcription again. Is there someone in it you did not notice at the time?',
    body: [
      { p: 'Now play it all back and listen for the parts you like the sound of. Not the cleverest parts. The parts where somebody real flickers past. This is panning for gold, and the ratio is supposed to be terrible: a lot of dirt, a little glitter. That is the deal you signed in the last lesson.' },
      { p: 'Whatever glitters, write it down. Keep every transcription, including the ones you have no use for. A scrap that reads as nothing in March has a way of starting a whole person in September. Your pile of transcriptions is an asset. It only grows.' },
      { p: 'Then work your gold. Take a fragment, clean it up, learn it by heart, and use it to launch the next improv. Pocket what you keep and go back for the next batch of dirt. Around and around: improvise, sift, transcribe, launch. The pile gets richer every pass, and the person in it gets more definite.' },
      { p: 'Once something is on paper, interrogate it with boring questions. What was this person doing ten minutes ago? What is in their pockets? How old are they, and what has that age done to the voice and the posture? The answers are not the point. The point is what the questions shake loose.' },
    ] },

  { id: 'ch-essence', module: 'piece', order: 7, movesFrom: 'ac-essence',
    title: 'The Person, Not the Props',
    attribution: 'Eric Bogosian',
    requiredReviewer: 'acting-professional',
    objective: 'Keep the character active and clear without decorating them.',
    orientation: 'A costume and a mimed steering wheel are two ways to hide the fact that nobody is home.',
    reflection: 'Watch your character with the sound off in your head. Is there anything to see?',
    body: [
      { p: 'Keep the character doing something. Standing, moving, working on the other person. And skip the decoration. No costume, no mime. The moment you mime driving a car, the audience starts grading your driving, and they stop watching the human being. The human being is the act. Everything else is a hat.' },
      { p: 'Want to know if it is working? Imagine playing it for a room that does not speak the language. Would they watch anyway? If the whole thing collapses without the words, there is no character yet. There is a script with a costume on. Notice this is the archetype test from earlier, pointed at your own work: the universal cast crosses every language barrier, and a built character should too.' },
      { p: 'Two more habits, and then the door. Let the character say the opposite of what the room expects, because nobody buys a ticket to hear your opinions recited. Give the worst person in the piece their side of the story; even the jerk thinks he is right, and he is more interesting the moment he gets to say why. Then hold everything you make to one final test: put on stage exactly what you would want to see if you were the one in the seat.' },
      { p: 'That is the loop, end to end: an empty room, a way in, no aiming, the recording, the transcript, and a person who is doing something. Now test what you found. Put the character into a scene, a script or a room full of other people, and find out what they cannot escape and what they are hiding. The person you discovered alone should survive contact with everyone else.' },
    ] },

  { id: 'ch-questions', module: 'piece', order: 8,
    attribution: 'Eric Bogosian',
    requiredReviewer: 'acting-professional',
    title: 'Questions the Improv Missed',
    objective: 'Ask the plain questions the improvisation never reached, and let the answers sharpen the character.',
    orientation: 'Improvising finds you a person. Questions find the rest of their life.',
    reflection: 'Take a character you are working on. Which of these questions could you not answer, and what did answering it change?',
    body: [
      { p: 'Improvising alone gets you a living person quickly, and it leaves holes. The character arrived talking, so you know their voice and their appetite, and you may have no idea where they were an hour ago or what they are carrying. Those holes show up in performance as vagueness. The cure is not more improvising. It is a short list of plain questions.' },
      { p: 'They are ordinary questions, the kind you would ask about any part:' },
      { list: [
        'What was this person doing ten minutes ago?',
        'What are they wearing, and what are they carrying?',
        'How old are they, and what does that do to their voice and their posture?',
        'Where are they: indoors or out, warm or cold, private or public?',
        'Who else is there, and what do they want from them?',
        'What happens to them after this is over?',
      ] },
      { p: 'Nothing here is scored and there are no right answers. The questions are there to jog your imagination, and the answers change what you do. Somebody who was sitting in a waiting room ten minutes ago walks in differently from somebody who was running. Somebody carrying something heavy speaks in shorter sentences. Answer out loud, in character, and you will hear which answers are true and which you are inventing to fill a gap.' },
      { p: 'Then improvise again with the answers in your body. Whatever still feels vague after that is worth watching, because an audience finds vagueness before it finds anything else.' },
    ] },

];

// One Library shelf per module, in path order.
const SHELF_ICON = { piece: '📝', commedia: '🃏', physical: '🚶' };
export const CHARACTER_COLLECTIONS = CHARACTER_MODULES.map(m => ({
  id: m.id, icon: SHELF_ICON[m.id], title: m.title,
  lessons: CHARACTER_LESSONS.filter(l => l.module === m.id && !l.reference)
    .sort((x, y) => x.order - y.order).map(l => l.id),
}));
